---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
import ProjectGallery from "../../components/ProjectGallery.astro";

const project = projects.find(
    p => p.slug === "trunnion-mounted-ball-valve"
);

if (!project) {
    throw new Error("Project not found");
}
---

<Layout>

<div class="project-page">

    <a class="back-link" href="/projects">
        ← Back to Projects
    </a>

<section class="project-header">

    <div class="project-summary">

        <img
            src={project.companyLogo}
            alt={`${project.company} logo`}
            class={`project-company-logo ${project.companyLogoClass}`}
        />

        <h1>{project.title}</h1>


    </div>

    <div class={`project-hero-image ${project.heroImageClass}`}>

        <img
            src={project.heroImage}
            alt={project.title}
            class="project-hero"
        />

    </div>

</section>

<hr class="experience-divider" />

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Overview
    </h3>

    <p>
        The project was initiated in response to customer feedback that the weight of one of our trunnion-
        mounted ball valve product lines was placing excessive loads on customer pipelines. The objective was
        to reduce the valve weight by 20% while maintaining compliance with the applicable design standards
        and requirements.
    </p>

    <p>
        I joined the project when the engineering work began, supporting the Design to Value team with the
        mechanical design activities. Working as part of a three-person engineering team, I was responsible
        for redesigning the DN 80 and DN 100 valve bodies across different pressure ratings. The team 
        ultimately achieved an average weight reduction of approximately 28% by transitioning from forged 
        to cast valve bodies, while retaining the existing internal components.
    </p>

</section>

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Project Development
    </h3>

    <p>
        The redesign focused exclusively on the valve bodies. All internal components, including the valve 
        trim, remained unchanged. The engineering work was divided among three Design to Value team 
        members, with each responsible for a selection of valve sizes. The challenge was to adapt the existing 
        forged body designs for casting while maintaining the applicable requirements for the DIN valve range.
    </p>

        <h4>Valve Body Redesign</h4>

    <p>
        The redesign required developing a new external geometry for the valve bodies while preserving the
        existing internal geometry and functional interfaces. This provided considerable design freedom to
        rethink the external shape and adapt it to the requirements of casting, rather than simply modifying
        the original forged design.
    </p>

    <p>
        The manufacturing approach varied depending on valve size: investment casting was used for smaller
        bodies, while larger bodies were designed for sand casting.
    </p>

    <p>
        I developed the DN 80 and DN 100 variants across different pressure ratings, creating the 3D CAD
        models and corresponding casting and manufacturing drawings. Throughout development, I
        iteratively evaluated the designs using the team's engineering calculation spreadsheet, with particular 
        attention to wall thicknesses and compliance with applicable design standards.
    </p>

    <p>
        My designs also served as a reference for the development of nearby valve sizes.
    </p>    

        <h4>Engineering Documentation and Design Review</h4>

    <p>
        The project required extensive engineering documentation, including CAD models and casting and
        manufacturing drawings for the different size and pressure-rating combinations. We uploaded our 
        deliverables to SAP, where they were submitted for review and approval by the subject matter expert 
        at the manufacturing site in Germany.
    </p>

    <p>
        Following completion of the engineering work, the project team travelled to Ettlingen, Germany, to 
        present the results and facilitate a design review with the stakeholders involved in the project.
    </p>

</section>

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Outcome
    </h3>

    <p>
        The project achieved an average valve weight reduction of approximately 28%, exceeding the original
        20% target. Its scope also expanded considerably beyond the initial project charter, growing from 24
        DIN valves to a delivery covering 33 DIN and 24 ASME valves.
    </p>

    <p>
        The broader project delivery included 392 engineering drawings, 172 released calculations and 1,164
        SAP part codes, alongside additional engineering activities.
    </p>

    <p>
        The team received a Celebrate Award in recognition of its contribution. The award highlighted the
        team's ability to deliver substantially more than originally expected, on time and with high-quality 
        engineering work.
    </p>

</section>

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Gallery
    </h3>

    <ProjectGallery
        images={[
            {
                src: "/images/projects/trunnion-mounted-ball-valve/gallery-1.jpg",
                alt: "Visual comparison of forged and casted valve bodies.",
                caption: "Casted (left) vs. forged (right) design"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/trunnion-mounted-ball-valve/gallery-2.jpg",
                alt: "Section of a manufacturing drawing.",
                caption: "Manufacturing drawing section"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/trunnion-mounted-ball-valve/gallery-3.jpg",
                alt: "First samples of casted valve bodies in wooden crate.",
                caption: "First samples"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/trunnion-mounted-ball-valve/gallery-4.jpg",
                alt: "Engineering team member presenting the trunnion-mounted ball valve redesign during a project review at Flowserve's Ettlingen facility in Germany.",
                caption: "Project design review"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/trunnion-mounted-ball-valve/gallery-5.jpg",
                alt: "Engineering team member presenting the trunnion-mounted ball valve redesign during a project review at Flowserve's Ettlingen facility in Germany.",
                caption: "Project design review"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/trunnion-mounted-ball-valve/gallery-6.jpg",
                alt: "Image of the written award.",
                caption: "Award"
            }
        ]}
    />

</section>

{project.references && (

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        References
    </h3>

    <div class="project-references">

        {project.references.map(reference => (

            <a
                href={reference.url}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
                {reference.text} →
            </a>

        ))}

    </div>

</section>

)}


</div>

</Layout>